            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Picks elements from each row (or column) of an array using that row's own list of positions. Where np.take uses one list of positions for every row, this takes a whole array of positions, shaped like the data, so each row can be reordered or sampled differently.\n\nIt is the partner of np.argsort, np.argmax and np.argmin used along an axis: they give positions row by row, and np.take_along_axis uses them to fetch the values - sorting every row of one array by the order of another, picking each joint's largest reading together with its time, keeping the three strongest channels per frame.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.take_along_axis <axis: int>\nDefault: axis -1 (along each row).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array to pick from. Every input is picked from and sent.\n\nindices:\nThe positions, held: send them first. They must have as many axes as the array; along the other axes their shape must match it (or be 1). -1 counts from the end;\npositions past the end are clipped to it.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way the positions run: -1 along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntaken:\nThe picked elements, shaped like the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTwo rows, 3 1 2 and 9 7 8. np.argsort gives each row's sorting order (1 2 0, the same for both), and np.take_along_axis uses it to fetch the values: 1 2 3 and 7 8 9 - each row sorted. The positions come from one array and are used on another, which is the point: sort the frames of a recording by the order of one joint's height, for example. A metro re-sends the array five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.take uses one list of positions for every row. np.argsort, np.argmax and np.argmin make positions. t.take_along_dim is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 960,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Picks elements from each row (or column) of an array using that row's own list of positions. Where np.take uses one list of positions for every row, this takes a whole array of positions, shaped like the data, so each row can be reordered or sampled differently.\n\nIt is the partner of np.argsort, np.argmax and np.argmin used along an axis: they give positions row by row, and np.take_along_axis uses them to fetch the values - sorting every row of one array by the order of another, picking each joint's largest reading together with its time, keeping the three strongest channels per frame.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.take_along_axis <axis: int>\nDefault: axis -1 (along each row).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array to pick from. Every input is picked from and sent.\n\nindices:\nThe positions, held: send them first. They must have as many axes as the array; along the other axes their shape must match it (or be 1). -1 counts from the end;\npositions past the end are clipped to it.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way the positions run: -1 along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntaken:\nThe picked elements, shaped like the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTwo rows, 3 1 2 and 9 7 8. np.argsort gives each row's sorting order (1 2 0, the same for both), and np.take_along_axis uses it to fetch the values: 1 2 3 and 7 8 9 - each row sorted. The positions come from one array and are used on another, which is the point: sort the frames of a recording by the order of one joint's height, for example. A metro re-sends the array five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.take uses one list of positions for every row. np.argsort, np.argmax and np.argmin make positions. t.take_along_dim is the PyTorch version."
        },
